# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EduLink environment
# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit .env.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Database: Postgres, two roles (README, "Setting up").
#   DATABASE_ADMIN_URL : the owner — migrations, grants, seeds, fixtures, jobs.
#   DATABASE_URL       : edulink_app — no ownership, no BYPASSRLS; every school
#                        table is behind row-level security (prisma/rls.sql).
# Migrations and seeding run as the owner; the app never does.
DATABASE_ADMIN_URL="postgresql://OWNER@localhost:5432/edulink_dev"
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://edulink_app:PASSWORD@localhost:5432/edulink_dev"
# Deployed: a pool of fifteen per server process (docs/runbooks/deploy.md, capacity;
# go-live.md 9.6). Every process (each web machine, the jobs machine) holds up to
# connection_limit connections, so processes x 15, plus about 10 for the release
# step and the owner's scripts and the 3 Postgres reserves, must stay under its
# max_connections (100 on a small managed Postgres: five processes, that is four
# web and the jobs one; more needs a pooler or a bigger Postgres). The load wave of
# 4 Oct 2026 never had more than five active at once; the limit is the CPU, so
# scale with processes, not a bigger pool. Without it Prisma takes cores x 2 + 1.
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://edulink_app:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/edulink?connection_limit=15&pool_timeout=20"
# (Prisma's own parameters: the scripts that hand a URL to psql or pg_dump strip them — scripts/pg-url.mjs.)

# Public origin. Used for magic links, invite links, push notification click
# targets and manifest start_url resolution.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Web Push (VAPID). REQUIRED — generate a pair with:  npm run vapid
# The app refuses to start in production without these, because the only
# symptom of an empty key is push notifications silently going nowhere.
# Public key is 87 chars, private is 43. Anything else is a truncated paste —
# and BOTH LINES MUST COME FROM THE SAME RUN: two keys of the right length from
# different runs pass every length check and then fail at every single send.
# The boot check derives the public key from the private one and compares.
# `npm run push:test` says which part is wrong without sending anything.
#
# ⚠ CHANGING THESE KEYS INVALIDATES EVERY EXISTING SUBSCRIPTION. A browser
# subscribes against one public key; rotate it and every device already signed
# up stops receiving, silently, until each person turns notifications off and on
# again. Treat rotation as a migration, not a config edit — see the README.
NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=""
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=""
# RFC 8292: "mailto:you@your-domain" or "https://your-domain". This is how a
# push service reaches you about misbehaving traffic, so it must be an address
# somebody reads — the placeholder below is a .local address and is refused in
# production.
VAPID_SUBJECT="mailto:admin@edulink.local"

# NOTE: there is deliberately no AUTH_SECRET. Sessions use opaque 32-byte
# random tokens stored as SHA-256 hashes, so there is no server-side signing
# key to configure — and a key nothing reads is a key that rots.

# Email transport.
#   "devbox" -> messages are written to the database and readable at /dev/mailbox
#   "smtp"   -> real mail through any SMTP server (SES, Postmark, a district relay).
#               Set the SMTP_* block below; a production boot with EMAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp
#               and an incomplete block refuses to start, and
#               `npm run email:test -- you@example.com` proves the wiring end to end.
EMAIL_TRANSPORT="devbox"
EMAIL_FROM="Shalaghar <no-reply@edulink.local>"

# Only read when EMAIL_TRANSPORT="smtp".
# SMTP_PORT defaults to 587 (STARTTLS); 465 implies SMTP_SECURE=true.
# SMTP_USER and SMTP_PASS go together — leave both empty for an open relay.
#SMTP_HOST="email-smtp.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com"
#SMTP_PORT="587"
#SMTP_SECURE="false"
#SMTP_USER=""
#SMTP_PASS=""

# SMS transport. Same shape as the email one above, and the same honesty:
#   "devbox" -> messages are written to the database and readable at /dev/mailbox
#              alongside the emails, so the whole password-reset flow works with
#              no provider account.
#   "twilio" -> real texts. Set the TWILIO_* block below; a production boot with
#              SMS_TRANSPORT=twilio and an incomplete block refuses to start, and
#              `npm run sms:test -- +15551234567` proves the wiring end to end.
#              ⚠ Sending to Indian numbers ALSO needs DLT-registered templates —
#              Twilio will accept a message the carrier then drops. See src/lib/sms.ts.
#   anything else -> not implemented; sms.ts THROWS rather than silently
#              dropping a reset code, which looks identical to a code that was
#              never asked for.
SMS_TRANSPORT="devbox"

# ── Fees ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE MONEY NEVER PASSES THROUGH THIS SERVER. A parent pays the school — into
# the school's own gateway account, or straight to the school's UPI id — and
# EduLink only creates the order and hears the webhook. There is no platform
# gateway account to configure here; each school enters its own keys on
# /admin/fees/settings, and those keys are sealed at rest with the one below.
#
# FEES_SECRET_KEY: 32 random bytes, base64 — `openssl rand -base64 32`. Without
# it no school can SAVE gateway keys (the settings page says so); UPI-id and
# counter payments never need it. ⚠ Losing it means every school re-enters its
# keys; changing it means the same. Keep it out of the database and the repo.
#FEES_SECRET_KEY=""
#
# FEES_GATEWAY_TRANSPORT:
#   "devbox"   -> online orders are pretend ones and /dev/payments settles them,
#                 so the whole checkout runs with no account anywhere (a laptop's
#                 default). A DEPLOYED server named "devbox" refuses to boot.
#   "razorpay" -> real orders at each school's own Razorpay account, webhooks at
#                 /api/webhooks/razorpay/<schoolId>.
#   "off", or unset on a DEPLOYED server -> online payment is off (the settings
#                 page says so, the checkout refuses); UPI-id and counter payments carry on.
FEES_GATEWAY_TRANSPORT="devbox"
# Almost nobody sets this: a stand-in for the Razorpay API in a test harness.
#FEES_GATEWAY_API_BASE="https://api.razorpay.com"
#
# ── Paid workshop seats: the COMPANY'S OWN gateway (decision 0004, amended 2026-10-02) ──
# Nothing of school fees comes here (decision 0003). The COMPANY COLLECTS: a
# guardian pays the company's Razorpay account for a seat past the pupil's
# allowance; at capture the payment's commission, the GST on it and the
# expert's net are fixed, and after the session plus 48 hours the expert is
# paid from the company's own bank, recorded by the operator on /root/gigs
# with a GST tax invoice. No Route, no linked accounts, no transfers: the
# adapter makes three calls — create an order, read a payment, refund one —
# and the webhook hears payment.captured, payment.failed and refund.failed.
# Read in src/lib/gig-gateway-env.ts.
#   unset                      -> "devbox" on a laptop: pretend orders, /dev/payments
#                                 settles them through the webhook's own path; on a
#                                 DEPLOYED server paid seats are OFF and free sessions carry on.
#   GATEWAY_PLATFORM_KEY_ID    -> the company's key id (rzp_live_… / rzp_test_…); with it
#                                 set, the two below are required (env.ts says so at boot,
#                                 fatal when deployed).
#   GATEWAY_PLATFORM_SECRET_ENC the key's secret SEALED under FEES_SECRET_KEY:
#                                 `FEES_SECRET_KEY=… npm run secret:seal -- gateway:platform:secret <secret>`
#                                 prints it (the key from the shell, not this file); the
#                                 plain secret never sits in this file.
#   GATEWAY_PLATFORM_WEBHOOK_SECRET what /api/webhooks/gateway/sessions checks signatures
#                                 with (register that URL for payment.captured,
#                                 payment.failed and refund.failed); under devbox, unset
#                                 means "devbox-sessions-webhook".
#                                 plans-pay (17 Oct 2026): the same secret signs the
#                                 passes' webhook, /api/webhooks/razorpay/platform (register
#                                 it too, for payment.captured, payment.failed, refund.failed).
# The kill switch itself is the operator's, on /root/gigs, not an env variable.
#GATEWAY_PLATFORM_KEY_ID=""
#GATEWAY_PLATFORM_SECRET_ENC=""
#GATEWAY_PLATFORM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
#GATEWAY_PLATFORM_API_BASE="https://api.razorpay.com"

# ── Texts to families (SMS and WhatsApp) ──────────────────────────────────────
# Reminders, absences and the office's notices reach families WITHOUT the app as
# texts. SMS goes through the SMS_TRANSPORT above; WhatsApp through the same
# Twilio account from a WhatsApp-enabled sender. Each school switches its own
# channels on at /admin/messaging; nothing is sent until it does.
#
# WHATSAPP_TRANSPORT: "devbox" (default; filed at /dev/mailbox) or "twilio".
WHATSAPP_TRANSPORT="devbox"
# The WhatsApp sender: the sandbox number while testing, the approved business
# number afterwards. "whatsapp:" prefix optional.
#TWILIO_WHATSAPP_FROM="whatsapp:+14155238886"
#
# ⚠ THE TEXTS ARE FIXED TEMPLATES (src/lib/message-templates.ts). An Indian SMS
# must match a template registered with the sender's DLT entity word for word,
# and a first WhatsApp message must be an approved content template. Print them
# for the forms with `npm run messages:templates`, register each language you
# will send, and put the ids here — DLT template ids under "sms", Twilio content
# SIDs under "whatsapp", by template key and language. Optional: without a
# WhatsApp SID the text goes as a plain body (only inside a 24-hour window the
# family opened); without a DLT id the carrier decides. docs/runbooks/messaging.md.
#MESSAGE_TEMPLATE_IDS='{"absence":{"sms":{"en":"1107..."},"whatsapp":{"en":"HX..."}}}'

# Only read when SMS_TRANSPORT="twilio".
# TWILIO_FROM is either a sending number in E.164 or a Messaging Service SID (MG…);
# an India deployment uses a Messaging Service tied to its DLT entity.
#TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID="ACxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
#TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=""
#TWILIO_FROM="+15551234567"
# Almost nobody sets this: Twilio regional endpoints, or a stand-in for sms:test.
#TWILIO_API_BASE="https://api.twilio.com"

# The country a bare mobile number belongs to.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE DEPLOYING OUTSIDE INDIA. Ten digits alone are not a phone
# number anywhere on earth — they are a phone number in a country. A parent
# typing `98765 43210` is stored as this code plus those digits, and `User.phone`
# is UNIQUE, so getting it wrong does not fail loudly: it files everybody under
# the wrong country and two spellings of one number become two accounts.
# Numbers typed WITH a country code (`+44…`, `0044…`) ignore this entirely.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODE="+91"

# Where uploads go (logos, avatars, attachments). Two choices:
#
#   this machine's disk — STORAGE_DIR, deliberately outside ./public so nothing
#   is web-root readable; files are served through an authorized route handler
#   instead. (§6) Right on a laptop, and on ONE machine with a persistent volume.
STORAGE_DIR="./storage"
#
#   a bucket — any S3-compatible store (AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2,
#   MinIO), which is what lets the web process be replaced or run on more than
#   one machine without moving files. ALL FIVE or none: a half-set block is
#   fatal when deployed, because the disk it would fall back to is the one the
#   next deploy throws away. NONE is fatal too on a deployed server on Fly (two
#   machines, no shared disk), and elsewhere unless STORAGE_DIR is the absolute
#   path of a mounted volume. No SDK — src/lib/s3.ts signs requests itself.
#   Originals go under <prefix>files/, resized copies under <prefix>variants/.
# STORAGE_BUCKET="edulink-uploads"
# STORAGE_S3_ENDPOINT="https://s3.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com"      # R2: https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# STORAGE_S3_REGION="ap-south-1"                                 # R2: auto
# STORAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
# STORAGE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""
# STORAGE_S3_PREFIX=""                # optional, e.g. "edulink/" to share a bucket
# STORAGE_S3_PATH_STYLE="true"        # "false" for virtual-hosted addressing

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Single sign-on (all optional — a provider's button appears on the login page
# only when its variables are ALL set; with none set, SSO is invisible).
#
# SSO signs people in; it never signs them up. An external identity that
# matches no existing account is refused with directions — accounts still come
# from invites, join codes and registration.
#
# ⚠ EACH PROVIDER IS ALL-OR-NOTHING. A provider exists only when every one of
# its variables is set; set some but not all and the sign-in button simply
# never appears, with nothing on screen explaining why. The boot check warns
# about a half-configured provider (never fatally — no SSO at all is a valid
# installation), and `npm run sso:check` prints the exact redirect URI this
# installation will send, fetches each provider's signing keys, mints Apple's
# client secret to prove the .p8 survived this file, and hands you a URL to
# finish the walk by hand.
#
# Redirect URI to register at each provider:
#   {NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL}/api/sso/google/callback     (Google Cloud Console)
#   {NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL}/api/sso/microsoft/callback  (Entra app registration)
#   {NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL}/api/sso/apple/callback      (Apple Service ID)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SSO_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
# SSO_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# SSO_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=""
# SSO_MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=""
# Tenant: "common" (any account), "organizations", "consumers", or a tenant id.
# SSO_MICROSOFT_TENANT="common"

# Apple needs the Service ID (not the App ID), your Team ID, the Key ID of a
# "Sign in with Apple" key, and that key's .p8 contents with newlines written
# as \n. Note: Apple's return trip is a cross-site POST, so it requires HTTPS
# in practice (the transient cookie is SameSite=None; Secure).
# SSO_APPLE_CLIENT_ID=""
# SSO_APPLE_TEAM_ID=""
# SSO_APPLE_KEY_ID=""
# SSO_APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----"

# ---- The phone (Phase 3) --------------------------------------------------
# Expo push: optional access token for exp.host (raises the rate ceiling and
# attributes the traffic). Without it, well-formed tokens are still accepted.
# EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN=""
# The deep-link scheme the SSO callback returns to when sign-in started from
# the app (…/api/sso/<provider>/start?client=app). Default: edulink
# APP_DEEP_LINK_SCHEME="edulink"

# ---- The release (Phase 6): the app's kill switch ---------------------------
# Read by GET /api/v1/app/version at every start of the phone app. All optional;
# the defaults refuse nobody. A malformed value is reported at boot (never
# fatally) and treated as unset. Versions are dotted: "1.4.2".
# The oldest build the API still serves; an older phone is sent to update. Default: 0.0.0
# APP_MIN_VERSION_IOS="0.0.0"
# APP_MIN_VERSION_ANDROID="0.0.0"
# The build in the stores now, so a newer phone can be nudged rather than stopped. Default: the minimum
# APP_LATEST_VERSION_IOS="0.0.0"
# APP_LATEST_VERSION_ANDROID="0.0.0"
# Where the update screen sends people. Default: none (the screen says to update, without a link)
# APP_STORE_URL_IOS="https://apps.apple.com/app/id0000000000"
# APP_STORE_URL_ANDROID="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=school.edulink.app"
# An operator's sentence for the update screen, replacing the standard one. Default: none
# APP_UPDATE_MESSAGE=""

# Where a person writes when their school cannot help — shown on /privacy and
# /delete-account as a mailto link. Optional: without it both pages send people
# to the school's office, which is the data fiduciary and the right first stop.
# SUPPORT_EMAIL="help@edulink.example"

# ---- The company's details (company-site, 16 Oct 2026) ---------------------------------------
# THE OWNER FILLS THESE. The home page (/) shows each one only once it is set, and nothing in its
# place until then; the operator's /root shows "Company details to fill" until the legal name, the
# registered office, the support email and the grievance officer's name and email are set.
# A value in the wrong shape (a CIN that is not 21 characters, a GSTIN that is not 15, an email
# without an @) is treated as unset. Lists separate their entries with "|".
# docs/runbooks/deploy.md, "Company details", says what each is and what the DPDP Act asks for.
# COMPANY_LEGAL_NAME=""
# COMPANY_BRAND_LINE=""
# COMPANY_CIN=""
# COMPANY_GSTIN=""
# COMPANY_REGISTERED_ADDRESS="line one|line two|City 400001|Maharashtra"
# COMPANY_CORRESPONDENCE_ADDRESS=""
# The support address for the home page and the sign-in page; SUPPORT_EMAIL above is used when this is unset.
# COMPANY_SUPPORT_EMAIL=""
# COMPANY_SUPPORT_PHONE=""
# COMPANY_GRIEVANCE_OFFICER_NAME=""
# COMPANY_GRIEVANCE_OFFICER_DESIGNATION=""
# COMPANY_GRIEVANCE_OFFICER_EMAIL=""
# COMPANY_FOUNDED_YEAR=""
# COMPANY_DIRECTORS="first director|second director"

# Signs the five-minute links the phone uses to open a PDF or Word file outside
# the app (src/lib/file-links.ts). Required where the app is deployed (boot
# refuses without it); in development, without it, a random key for the
# process signs them. At least 32 random characters; rotating it only expires
# the links in flight.
# FILE_LINK_SECRET=

# Behind a proxy that is not Fly, the Fly-Client-IP header is whatever the
# client sent and is ignored; the rate limits key on the last X-Forwarded-For
# hop. Set to 1 only when your own proxy sets Fly-Client-IP itself. On Fly
# (FLY_APP_NAME set by the platform) the header is trusted without this.
# TRUST_FLY_CLIENT_IP="0"

# Behind Cloudflare's proxy the hop that reaches the app is a Cloudflare edge,
# shared by everyone routed through it. Set to 1 when the proxy is on: a request
# whose hop is inside Cloudflare's published ranges (src/lib/cloudflare.ts) is
# then keyed on CF-Connecting-IP; any other request on its own address, so the
# header cannot be forged by reaching the origin directly. Off by default.
# TRUST_CLOUDFLARE="0"

# ---- Retention: the nightly sweep (retention:sweep, 04:00) ----------------------
# How long the security-context rows stay after they stop mattering, in days.
# All optional; a malformed value is reported at boot and the default stands.
# Sessions after they expire or are revoked. Default: 7
# RETENTION_SESSION_DAYS="7"
# Sign-in tokens after they expire or are used. Default: 7
# RETENTION_TOKEN_DAYS="7"
# Rate-limit buckets after their window closes. Default: 1
# RETENTION_RATE_LIMIT_DAYS="1"
# Phone upload tickets after they are spent (unspent ones are files:reclaim's). Default: 30
# RETENTION_UPLOAD_TICKET_DAYS="30"
# The dev mailbox's sent emails. Default: 30
# RETENTION_OUTBOX_DAYS="30"
# Job run history; each job's latest successful run is always kept. Default: 90
# RETENTION_JOB_RUN_DAYS="90"
# Audit log rows (docs/decisions/0002, rows 22 and 24). 0 keeps them forever. Default: 730
# RETENTION_AUDIT_DAYS="730"
# Which staff opened a pupil's health card (the guardian's list shows 90 days; a year
# keeps a window for a dispute). From 90 to 3650. Default: 365
# RETENTION_HEALTH_READS_DAYS="365"

# Adds `preload` to the HSTS header in production. Browsers keep this promise for
# months after it is withdrawn: set it only once the production domain is fixed
# and every subdomain of it is on HTTPS.
# HSTS_PRELOAD=1

# Error tracking (2026-10-01). When set, every unexpected error a /api/v1
# handler answers 500 with, and every page render error, is posted to this
# Sentry-compatible DSN by a plain fetch (src/lib/metrics.ts: no SDK) — the
# route pattern, the error's name, message and frames, and the person's and
# the school's ids as tags; never a body, header, cookie or query string.
# Unset, the errors go to the server log as before. /root/metrics says which.
# SENTRY_DSN=

# ---- The exam cell's Academic Bank of Credits (ABC) push ---------------------
# There is no real ABC connection yet (docs/college.md: the transport is a stub).
# POST /api/v1/exam-cell/credits/push answers 409 "The Academic Bank is not
# connected." unless this is set; with it, each pending credit row is marked
# SENT with an ABC-STUB-… reference and nothing leaves the server. Set it on a
# development machine (the API sweep then answers the door 200); never on a
# deployed server, where a stub reference would read as a real filing.
# ABC_STUB_SEND=1

# ---- The assistant: local model endpoints and the corpus (Phase A) ----------
# docs/plans/assistant.md; docs/runbooks/assistant-local.md. Two OpenAI-compatible
# endpoints, local first: scripts/model-serve.sh starts llama.cpp's llama-server
# with Qwen3-30B-A3B (chat, :8180) and Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B (embeddings, :8181),
# both on 127.0.0.1. The URLs end at the API root; the model names are the
# servers' --alias. corpus:ingest, help:index and assistant:ask refuse to start
# without them (src/lib/assistant-env.ts). A hosted endpoint is decision 0005's
# (Phase B), never set here first: what a person types would leave the machine.
# ASSISTANT_MODEL_URL="http://127.0.0.1:8180/v1"
# ASSISTANT_MODEL="qwen3-30b-a3b"
# ASSISTANT_EMBED_URL="http://127.0.0.1:8181/v1"
# ASSISTANT_EMBED_MODEL="qwen3-embedding-0.6b"
# Optional bearer keys, for a host that wants one; the local servers need none.
# ASSISTANT_MODEL_KEY=
# ASSISTANT_EMBED_KEY=
# The corpus fetcher's User-Agent names this contact address to the book sites
# (scripts/corpus-fetch.ts); CORPUS_DIR moves storage/corpus; CORPUS_PAUSE_MS is
# the pause between two requests to one host (1500 by default).
# CORPUS_CONTACT="owner@example.org"
# CORPUS_DIR=
# CORPUS_PAUSE_MS=1500

# ---- The assistant service: switches, caps, retention (Phase B, decision 0005) --
# ASSISTANT_ENABLED=0 is the kill switch: every school's assistant closes at once
# and nothing is sent to the model (the doors answer 403 and say so).
# ASSISTANT_DAILY_CAP is a person's questions in a school day (20 by default; 40 until 12 October 2026);
# ASSISTANT_MONTHLY_TOKENS a school's tokens in a calendar month (0, no budget, by default since 12 October 2026).
# RETENTION_ASSISTANT_DAYS is how long a conversation is kept after its last
# message (30; 1 to 365); RETENTION_ASSISTANT_USAGE_DAYS the daily counts (62).
# ASSISTANT_ENABLED=1
# ASSISTANT_DAILY_CAP=20
# ASSISTANT_MONTHLY_TOKENS=0
# RETENTION_ASSISTANT_DAYS="30"
# RETENTION_ASSISTANT_USAGE_DAYS="62"

# website-builder B (15 Oct 2026): school websites (the WEBSITE module) and their own domains.
# A school's website lives ONLY at the school's own domain (16 Oct 2026): there is no platform subdomain.
# SITE_CNAME_TARGET  where a school's www record points: the sites app's own name. Default "sites.shalaghar.com".
# APP_HOSTS    the app's OWN hosts, comma-separated, each with its port where it has one. Any other named host
#              may be a school's domain (the middleware rewrites it to /sites/by-host/<host>, which serves the site
#              only for a LIVE domain and 404s anything else). Unset in development it is the line below, and
#              <slug>.localhost:3000 serves that school's published site; UNSET IN PRODUCTION NO host is a site and
#              no school's domain is served — set it to the app's public host(s), e.g. "app.shalaghar.in,edulink-web.fly.dev".
#              A bare IP address, a name with no dot, and *.internal / *.flycast are never site hosts.
# SITE_APEX_IP an IPv4 address a school's bare domain (example.in) may point an A record at (the sites app's
#              shared IPv4). Unset, the domain page tells the school to forward the bare domain to www.
# FLY_API_TOKEN, FLY_SITES_APP  the token and the Fly app the sites are served from, for each school domain's
#              certificate (docs/runbooks/deploy.md, "School websites and their own domains"). Unset, as in
#              development, no certificate is asked for; a domain whose DNS is right goes live here without one,
#              and in production stays "Verifying" until both are set.
SITE_CNAME_TARGET="sites.shalaghar.com"
APP_HOSTS="localhost:3000,127.0.0.1:3000"
#SITE_APEX_IP=""
#FLY_API_TOKEN=""
#FLY_SITES_APP=""
# end website-builder B

# meta-posting (16 Oct 2026)
# ---- A school's Facebook Page and Instagram account (posting school-wide posts there) --------------------
# META_APP_ID, META_APP_SECRET  the platform's Meta app (developers.facebook.com, docs/runbooks/meta-posting.md):
#              its App ID and App Secret. UNSET, there is no Meta here: /admin/settings/social says the platform
#              has not configured it and offers nothing, the composer shows no Facebook or Instagram box, and the
#              OAuth routes answer 404. The app's Valid OAuth Redirect URI is <NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL>/api/social/meta/callback.
#              The Page tokens are sealed under FEES_SECRET_KEY (above); without it nothing can be connected.
# META_GRAPH_VERSION  the Graph API version every call names. Default "v23.0"; move it forward before Meta
#              retires it (about two years after release).
# Instagram fetches a post's picture from <NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL>/api/files/…?social=…, so the app's address must be
# reachable from the internet for Instagram (Facebook uploads the picture itself).
#META_APP_ID=""
#META_APP_SECRET=""
#META_GRAPH_VERSION="v23.0"
# end meta-posting
